es-rgds-screensaver: the screensaver on the top panel, with a backdrop behind its text

The dual-screen themes run ES on a canvas three panels wide (1920x480 on the RG DS, 3072x768 on the Plus), and
es-rgds-uiwidth.patch sizes the menus against one panel (ES_UI_WIDTH). The screensaver was still sized against the
whole canvas:

- A slide was fitted to the canvas' height and centred on the canvas, which put it on the bottom panel. A slide wider
  than the panel (anything wider than 4:3, and most screenshots with a bezel) ran over the panel's edges, onto the top
  screen on one side and off the canvas on the other. A long game name did the same.
- The date and time (SHOW DATE TIME) sat in the canvas' top-left corner, on the top screen, while the slide was on
  the bottom one.
- A decoration's (bezel's) frame and its .info viewport were fitted to the canvas.

The screensaver now takes the top panel: the slide, its game and system names or marquee, the decoration and the
clock are all laid out inside it, and the bottom screen stays black. A device propped open with the top screen up
shows the slideshow and the time like a desk clock. On a one-panel layout (any other theme, ES_UI_WIDTH unset or the
screen's width) the panel is the whole screen, so nothing changes there.

The clock and the game's name also get a soft backdrop: a rounded half-transparent box behind the date and time, and
one behind the game and system names, each as wide as its longer line, so the text stays readable over a light
slide. The names' box fades with them. It is a switch in both screensaver menus (TEXT BACKDROP,
ScreenSaverTextBackdrop), on by default.

